WEBVTT

00:00:00.249 --> 00:00:03.400
We know humans learn
from their past experiences

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:06.700
and machines follow
instructions given by humans.

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:10.408
But what if humans can train
the machines to learn from

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:13.800
their past data and do what
humans can do at much faster?

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.500
Well, that's called machine learning,
but it's a lot more than just learning.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.000
It's also about
understanding and reasoning.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.500
So today we will learn about
the basics of machine learning.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.500
So that's Paul, he loves
listening to new songs.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
He either likes them or dislikes them.

00:00:31.001 --> 00:00:34.500
Paul decides this on the
basis of the song's tempo,

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:38.500
genre, intensity, and the gender of voice.

00:00:38.999 --> 00:00:42.500
For simplicity, let's just use
tempo and intensity for now.

00:00:42.849 --> 00:00:47.500
So here, tempo is on the x-axis,
ranging from relaxed to fast,

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:52.500
whereas intensity is on the
y-axis ranging from light to soaring.

00:00:52.574 --> 00:00:58.500
We see that Paul likes the song
with fast tempo and soaring intensity,

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:03.500
while he dislikes the song with
relaxed tempo and light intensity.

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:05.500
So now we know Paul's choices.

00:01:05.599 --> 00:01:07.500
Let's say Paul listens to a new song.

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:09.500
Let's name it a song A.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:12.500
Song A has fast tempo
and a soaring intensity.

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:14.500
So it lies somewhere here.

00:01:14.649 --> 00:01:18.500
Looking at the data, can you guess
whether Paul will like the song or not?

00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:19.500
Correct.

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:21.000
So Paul likes the song.

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:23.691
By looking at Paul's
past choices, we were

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:26.500
able to classify the
unknown song very easily.

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:27.500
Right?

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:30.000
Let's say, now Paul listens to a new song.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:32.000
Let's label it as song B.

00:01:32.199 --> 00:01:38.000
So song B lies somewhere here with
medium tempo and medium intensity.

00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:42.000
Neither relaxed nor fast,
neither light nor soaring.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.500
Now can you guess
whether Paul likes it or not?

00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:47.500
Not able to guess whether
Paul will like it or dislike it.

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:49.000
Are the choices unclear?

00:01:49.199 --> 00:01:50.000
Correct.

00:01:50.001 --> 00:01:52.000
We could easily classify song A.

00:01:52.324 --> 00:01:56.000
But when the choice became
complicated as in the case of song B.

00:01:56.001 --> 00:01:57.000
Yes.

00:01:57.001 --> 00:01:59.000
And that's where machine learning comes in.

00:01:59.001 --> 00:02:00.000
Let's see how.

00:02:00.001 --> 00:02:04.000
In the same example, for song B, if
we draw a circle around the song B,

00:02:04.199 --> 00:02:07.000
we see that there are four words for like.

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:09.000
Whereas one word for dislike.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:13.000
If we go for the majority words, we can say
that Paul will definitely like the song.

00:02:13.001 --> 00:02:14.000
That's all.

00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:16.000
This was a basic machine
learning algorithm also.

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:18.000
It's called gainierous neighbors.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.000
So this is just a small example in one of
the many machine learning algorithms.

00:02:23.001 --> 00:02:24.000
Quite easy, right?

00:02:24.001 --> 00:02:26.000
Believe me, it is.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.000
But what happens when the choices become
complicated as in the case of song B?

00:02:31.075 --> 00:02:33.000
That's when machine learning comes in.

00:02:33.001 --> 00:02:35.000
It learns the data, builds
the prediction model.

00:02:35.001 --> 00:02:39.000
And when the new data point
comes in, it can easily protect for it.

00:02:39.275 --> 00:02:43.000
More the data, better the
model, higher will be the accuracy.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.000
There are many ways in
which the machine learns.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.452
It could be either
supervised learning and

00:02:48.476 --> 00:02:51.007
supervised learning or
reinforcement learning.

00:02:51.008 --> 00:02:54.000
Let's first quickly understand
supervised learning.

00:02:54.001 --> 00:02:58.000
Suppose your friend gives you 1
million coins of 3 different currencies.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.000
Say 1 rupee, 1 euro, and 1 derham.

00:03:01.001 --> 00:03:03.000
Each coin has different weights.

00:03:03.001 --> 00:03:06.515
For example, a coin of 1
rupee weighs 3 grams, 1

00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:10.000
euro weighs 7 grams, and
1 derham weighs 4 grams.

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:12.000
Your model will predict
the currency of the coin.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.000
Here, your weight
becomes the feature of coins.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.000
While currency becomes the label.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.000
When you feed this data to the
machine learning model, it learns.

00:03:21.001 --> 00:03:24.000
Which feature is
associated with which label?

00:03:24.001 --> 00:03:29.000
For example, it will learn that if a coin
is of 3 grams, it will be a 1 rupee coin.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.000
Let's give a new coin to the machine.

00:03:31.001 --> 00:03:35.000
On the basis of the weight of the new
coin, your model will predict the currency.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:39.000
Hence, supervised learning uses
labeled data to train the model.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.872
Here, the machine knew
the features of the object

00:03:41.873 --> 00:03:45.000
and also the labels
associated with those features.

00:03:45.001 --> 00:03:48.000
On this note, let's move to unsupervised
learning and see the difference.

00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:51.000
Suppose you have cricket
data set of various players

00:03:51.275 --> 00:03:54.000
with their respective
scores and the wickets taken.

00:03:54.125 --> 00:03:56.874
When you feed this data
set to the machine, the

00:03:56.898 --> 00:04:00.007
machine identifies the
pattern of player performance.

00:04:00.008 --> 00:04:03.000
So it plots this data with the
respective wickets and the x-axis

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:05.000
while runs on the y-axis.

00:04:05.001 --> 00:04:08.000
While looking at the data, you will
clearly see that there are 2 clusters.

00:04:08.001 --> 00:04:13.000
The one cluster are the players who
scored high runs and took less wickets.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:18.000
While the other cluster is of the players
who scored less runs but took many wickets.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.000
So here will be interpret these 2
clusters as batsmen and bowlers.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.000
The important point to note here is that
there are no labels of batsmen and bowlers.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:31.000
Hence, the learning with unlabeled
data is unsupervised learning.

00:04:31.001 --> 00:04:33.910
So we saw supervised learning
where the data was labeled

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:37.012
and the unsupervised learning
where the data was undabeled.

00:04:37.125 --> 00:04:41.000
And then there is reinforcement
learning which is a reward based learning.

00:04:41.001 --> 00:04:43.026
Or we can say that it works
on the principle of feedback.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.484
Here, let's say you
provide the system with

00:04:45.508 --> 00:04:48.012
an image of a dog
and ask it to identify it.

00:04:48.013 --> 00:04:51.000
The system identifies it as a cat.

00:04:51.001 --> 00:04:54.000
So you give a negative feedback to the
machine saying that it's a dog's image.

00:04:54.001 --> 00:04:56.280
The machine will learn
from the feedback and

00:04:56.304 --> 00:04:58.759
finally if it comes across
any other image of a

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.000
dog, it will be able
to classify it correctly.

00:05:01.001 --> 00:05:03.000
That is reinforcement learning.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.000
To generalize machine learning
model, let's see a flow chart.

00:05:06.001 --> 00:05:08.512
Input is given to a machine
learning model which then

00:05:08.536 --> 00:05:11.012
gives the output according
to the algorithm applied.

00:05:11.013 --> 00:05:15.000
If it's right, we take the
output as a final result.

00:05:15.001 --> 00:05:17.400
Else, we provide
feedback to the training

00:05:17.424 --> 00:05:20.007
model and ask it to
predict and let's learn.

00:05:20.008 --> 00:05:23.000
I hope you've understood
supervised and unsupervised learning.

00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:25.000
So let's have a quick quiz.

00:05:25.001 --> 00:05:27.680
You have to determine
whether the given scenarios

00:05:27.704 --> 00:05:30.012
use a supervised or
unsupervised learning.

00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:33.358
Simple right, scenario one,
Facebook recognizes your

00:05:33.382 --> 00:05:37.012
friend in a picture from an
album of tagged photographs.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:43.000
scenario two, Netflix recommends new movies
based on someone's past movie choices.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.122
scenario three, analyzing
bank data for suspicious

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:50.012
transactions and
flagging fraud transactions.

00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:53.000
Think wisely and comment
below your answers.

00:05:53.001 --> 00:05:55.400
Moving on, don't you
sometimes wonder how

00:05:55.424 --> 00:05:58.007
is machine learning
possible in today's era?

00:05:58.008 --> 00:06:01.000
Well that's because today we
have humongous data available.

00:06:01.275 --> 00:06:06.000
Everybody's online either making a
transaction or just serving the internet.

00:06:06.001 --> 00:06:09.000
And that's generating a huge
amount of data every minute.

00:06:09.125 --> 00:06:12.000
And that data my friend
is the key to analysis.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.834
Also, the memory handling capabilities
of computers have largely increased,

00:06:16.858 --> 00:06:21.012
which helps them to process such a huge
amount of data at hand without any delay.

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:25.000
And yes, computers now have
great computational powers.

00:06:25.001 --> 00:06:28.000
So there are a lot of applications
of machine learning out there.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.165
To name a few, machine
learning is used in healthcare

00:06:31.189 --> 00:06:34.007
where diagnostics are
predicted for doctor's review.

00:06:34.008 --> 00:06:37.036
The sentiment analysis that the
tech giants are doing on social

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.007
media is another interesting
application of machine learning.

00:06:40.008 --> 00:06:42.514
Fraud detection in the
finance sector and also to

00:06:42.538 --> 00:06:45.007
predict customer churn
in the e-commerce sector.

00:06:45.008 --> 00:06:48.517
While booking a gap, you must
have encountered surge pricing

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:52.012
often where it says the fair
of your trip has been updated.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:56.000
Continue booking.
Yes, please, I'm getting late for office.

00:06:56.001 --> 00:06:58.958
Well, that's an interesting
machine learning model

00:06:58.982 --> 00:07:02.012
which is used by global
taxi giant Uber and others.

00:07:02.013 --> 00:07:05.855
Where they have differential
pricing in real time based on

00:07:05.879 --> 00:07:10.000
demand, the number of cars
available, bad weather, rush R etc.

00:07:10.001 --> 00:07:15.012
So they use the surge pricing model to
ensure that those who need a gap can get one.

00:07:15.013 --> 00:07:20.000
Also, it uses predictive modeling to
predict where the demand will be high.

00:07:20.001 --> 00:07:24.000
That drivers can take care of the demand
and surge pricing can be minimized.

00:07:24.001 --> 00:07:28.000
Great. Hey Siri, can you remind
me to book a cab at 6pm today?

00:07:28.001 --> 00:07:30.000
Ok, I'll remind you.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.000
Thanks. No problem.

